 (md removed)←RemoveHtmlTags markdown;pattern;lines;fenced;i;line;IsFence
⍝ Removes all HTML tags from `markdown`: injecting HTML is not permitted on a home page.
⍝ These are left alone:
⍝ * Automatic links like <https://tatin.dev>, <mailto:kai@aplteam.com> and <kai@aplteam.com>
⍝ * Everything between a pair of fences, no matter whether ``` or ~~~ is used
⍝ * Everything MarkAPL considers to be inline code
⍝ Code is left alone because MarkAPL escapes the pointy brackets in code anyway: what the
⍝ reader gets to see is then the tag itself rather than its effect, so it can do no harm.
⍝ `removed` is 1 if at least one HTML tag was removed, otherwise 0. That allows the caller
⍝ to tell the user rather than changing her text behind her back.
 pattern←''
 pattern,←⊂'<(?:https?|ftp|file)://[^>]*>'          ⍝ Automatic links: those should survive untouched
 pattern,←⊂'<mailto:[^>]*>'                         ⍝ ... "mailto:" has no "//"
 pattern,←⊂'<[^<>[:space:]]+@[^<>[:space:]]+>'      ⍝ ... and a bare email address is a link, too
 pattern,←⊂'<[^>]*>'                                ⍝ Fetches HTML tags - not allowed!
⍝ This is how MarkAPL itself decides what opens and closes a code block; see `GetListBlock`
 IsFence←{3>≢⍵~' ':0 ⋄ (⊂3↑⍵~' ')∊'```' '~~~'}
⍝ Dropping exactly the two characters of the delimiter rather than removing all CRs and LFs
⍝ keeps this reversible, so `removed` cannot be fooled by a lonely LF within a line.
 lines←2↓¨{(CRLF⍷⍵)⊂⍵}CRLF,markdown
 fenced←0
 :For i :In ⍳≢lines
     line←i⊃lines
     :If IsFence line
         fenced←~fenced
     :ElseIf ~fenced
         (i⊃lines)←pattern RemoveHtmlTags_ line
     :EndIf
 :EndFor
 md←2↓∊(⊂CRLF),¨lines
 removed←md≢markdown
⍝Done
